/* ===== final ===== */
FINAL = {
  title:"Final challenge",
  how:"Thirty hypothetical situations drawn from the chapter and its two application supplements: every company, workshop, and public body described here is invented for practice, and none of them is a real organization. Work out what is actually happening before you answer, then read every explanation, including the ones for options you did not choose.",
  questions:[

    {q:"A hypothetical instrument maker, Kestrel Instruments, takes every final order over the telephone. Its website carries a configurator that helps a buyer specify a machine, and a portal where owners book repairs and download manuals. The sales director says the firm therefore does no electronic commerce at all. What does the chapter say?",
     opts:[
       "He is right, because the money changes hands on the phone, and electronic commerce is defined by where the payment is taken",
       "He is wrong: electronic commerce covers the events leading up to a purchase and the service after it, not the checkout alone",
       "He is right about the configurator but wrong about the repair portal, since only after-sales contact is counted as electronic commerce",
       "He is wrong, because any firm that operates a website of its own is by definition trading in an electronic marketplace, whatever the site does"],
     a:1, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "The chapter defines electronic commerce as electronic transactions over digital networks, and it extends the term past the moment of payment to what surrounds that moment.",
       "Correct. The chapter says electronic commerce goes beyond consumers merely buying and selling products online: it can involve the events leading up to the purchase of a product as well as customer service after the sale.",
       "Both halves belong. Helping a buyer specify a machine before the order and supporting the owner afterwards are each named by the chapter as part of electronic commerce.",
       "Owning a website is not the test. The electronic marketplace is the chapter&rsquo;s name for the space of services and functionality that trading online opens up, not a label earned by publishing a page."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical seed merchant, Marbury Seedhouse, moves from a printed catalog to a website. Within a year it also offers sowing calendars, germination advice by crop, saved order histories, and a subscription that ships at the right week. Its founder asks what the chapter calls the space that moving online opened up, in which all of that could be offered.",
     opts:[
       "Disintermediation, because the printed catalog that used to carry the range has been removed from the chain of supply",
       "The platform business model, because the site now enables transactions between the merchant and each of its customers",
       "The electronic marketplace, in which a virtually limitless array of services, features and functionality can be offered",
       "Business-to-business commerce, because a subscription that ships to a schedule is a supply arrangement rather than an ordinary retail sale"],
     a:2, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "Disintermediation is the removal of an intermediary such as a wholesaler or retailer. A catalog is a sales channel the merchant owned, not a middleman standing between it and its buyers.",
       "A platform business model enables transactions between other parties, as an auction site does between two people. Here the merchant is itself one of the two parties in every sale.",
       "Correct. The chapter says electronic commerce has led to the creation of an electronic marketplace where a virtually limitless array of services, features, and functionality can be offered. The term names that shared trading space rather than any one merchant&rsquo;s site, and the seedhouse has moved into it.",
       "Business-to-business commerce is trade among businesses, such as sourcing materials from suppliers. A grower who subscribes for their own plot is still an end consumer."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical bearing manufacturer, Cordwell Bearings, has always sold through industrial distributors. It is about to sell direct from its own site and keep the distributor margin for itself. Its operations manager warns the board that the margin does not arrive on its own. What is she pointing at?",
     opts:[
       "That cutting out the distributor also moves the distributor&rsquo;s work &mdash; finding buyers, taking orders, handling returns &mdash; onto the maker",
       "That selling direct is really re-intermediation, so a new intermediary will appear and take a similar share of every order",
       "That the distributor&rsquo;s margin was never truly available, since a maker selling direct is obliged to hold the distributor&rsquo;s prices",
       "That an online presence is optional for a manufacturer, so the safer course is to leave the existing distribution arrangement exactly as it is"],
     a:0, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "Correct. Disintermediation is cutting out the middleman and reaching customers directly; the chapter&rsquo;s examples are airlines selling to travelers and the rise of direct-to-consumer brands. The margin comes attached to the tasks the distributor was performing.",
       "Re-intermediation describes a new intermediary entering, such as a marketplace. Nothing here forces one to appear, because the maker is choosing to reach buyers itself.",
       "Nothing in the chapter obliges a direct seller to hold the old price. The warning is about work and cost, not about what the maker is permitted to charge.",
       "The chapter argues the opposite. Because of the potential for increased market reach and scalability, an online presence has become a strategic necessity for most companies."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical ceramics workshop, Halloway Ceramics, stopped selling through galleries and now sells from its own site, but almost nobody finds it. It lists on a large online marketplace, sales recover, and the marketplace takes a share of every order. What has happened, in the chapter&rsquo;s terms?",
     opts:[
       "The workshop has enlarged its own electronic marketplace, because a marketplace listing adds services and features to what the workshop can offer buyers",
       "The workshop has moved from business-to-consumer selling into consumer-to-consumer selling, because a marketplace mainly hosts individual sellers",
       "The workshop is now conducting business-to-business trade, because its selling agreement is with the marketplace operator rather than with the buyer",
       "It cut out one intermediary and took on another: the marketplace runs a platform business model and now sits between it and its buyers"],
     a:3, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "The electronic marketplace is the chapter&rsquo;s term for the space of services and functionality that trading online opens up, not an asset a seller owns and enlarges by adding a channel.",
       "Consumer-to-consumer describes transactions between people not necessarily working together. A workshop selling its own output is a business on one side of every sale it makes.",
       "The sale that matters is still made to an end consumer. Paying a fee to a marketplace does not convert a retail transaction into trade between two businesses.",
       "Correct. Disintermediation removed the gallery, and the marketplace is a new intermediary. The chapter notes that online marketplaces such as auction and craft sites use a platform business model, enabling transactions between other parties."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical trade body is briefing new members on where the money in electronic commerce actually sits. One member argues that consumer retail must be the largest form of it, because consumer retail is the part of it everyone sees. What does the chapter report?",
     opts:[
       "Consumer retail is indeed the largest, and trade between businesses is a small specialized corner of electronic commerce",
       "The two are roughly equal in size, because almost every consumer-facing firm also buys from its own suppliers online",
       "Business-to-business trade is by far the largest form by revenue, on the manufacturing and wholesale figures the chapter reports",
       "Consumer-to-consumer trade is the largest, because auction and resale sites carry far more separate transactions than ordinary retailers do"],
     a:2, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "The chapter puts it the other way round, and notes that many firms concentrate solely on trade with other businesses rather than on consumers at all.",
       "Consumer-facing firms do buy from suppliers online, which the chapter mentions, but that observation does not make the two halves equal in revenue.",
       "Correct. The chapter says business-to-business commerce is by far the largest form in terms of revenues, citing manufacturing shipment and wholesale sales figures for a single reported year.",
       "Counting transactions is not the same as counting revenue. Trade between consumers involves many small sales, and the chapter does not present it as the largest by value."]},

    {q:"Two hypothetical collectors, neither of them a business, agree the sale of a vintage concert poster through an online auction site. The site handles the listing, the messaging and its own fee, but it never owns the poster. How does the chapter classify the sale, and the site?",
     opts:[
       "A consumer-to-consumer sale, with the site running a platform business model that enables the transaction between the two people",
       "A business-to-consumer sale, with the site as the seller, because the buyer&rsquo;s contract is formed on the site&rsquo;s own pages",
       "A business-to-business sale, because both collectors are acting commercially the moment money changes hands for goods",
       "Not electronic commerce at all, since the chapter restricts the term to transactions in which at least one party is a firm"],
     a:0, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "Correct. The chapter defines consumer-to-consumer commerce as transactions between people not necessarily working together, and notes that online marketplaces such as auction and craft sites use a platform business model.",
       "The site is not a party to this sale. Business-to-consumer describes a transaction between a business and its own customer, which is not what a listing fee creates.",
       "Business-to-business commerce is trade among businesses, such as a manufacturer dealing with its suppliers. Two private collectors do not become firms by exchanging money once.",
       "The chapter&rsquo;s definition covers transactions among firms, between firms and their customers, and between customers, so a sale between two people is squarely inside it."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical county puts three services online in the same year: an application for identity cards, a portal publishing its spending data, and a form that lets builders file plans. A councillor asks whether any of this counts as electronic commerce.",
     opts:[
       "None of it does, because no goods change hands and the county is not selling anything to anybody at any stage of these three services",
       "All of it is business-to-consumer commerce, because a resident who applies for an identity card is in every sense a customer of the county",
       "Only the plans form counts, because builders are businesses, and the two services aimed at individual residents fall outside the chapter&rsquo;s definitions",
       "It is e-government: information systems giving citizens, organizations and agencies information about public services and letting them interact with government"],
     a:3, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "The chapter treats this as a related form that does not involve goods or services sold by or to companies, and it still gives that form a name of its own.",
       "The chapter keeps government services separate from business-to-consumer commerce, which describes transactions between businesses and their own customers.",
       "The chapter&rsquo;s examples include online applications for identity cards, municipal services, electronic voting, and electronic access to public government data.",
       "Correct. E-government is the chapter&rsquo;s term for using information systems to provide citizens, organizations and other governmental agencies with information about public services and to allow interaction with the government."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical firm, Camlet Credit, does one thing: it advances money against unpaid invoices for small manufacturers, entirely online, and offers nothing else. A bank manager says it cannot be a serious financial business, because it has no current accounts, no cards and no branches. What does the chapter&rsquo;s account of fintech say?",
     opts:[
       "The bank manager is right, because the chapter treats fintech firms as suppliers of technology to banks rather than as providers of financial services themselves",
       "Focusing on one service and doing it very well is the pattern the chapter describes, and reaching borrowers directly bypasses the traditional financial intermediary",
       "The firm is not doing fintech but electronic commerce, because lending money is a service sold to a customer like any other product",
       "Breadth is the test the chapter applies, so a firm has to match a bank&rsquo;s range of products before its technology is described as fintech"],
     a:1, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "The chapter names online-only banks, mobile payment processors, peer-to-peer lenders, small-business lenders and digital investment managers as fintech firms serving customers directly.",
       "Correct. The chapter says that whereas traditional banks provide a wide range of services, fintech companies typically focus on one service and do this very well, and that fintechs can bypass traditional financial intermediaries to reach consumers directly.",
       "The chapter places online financial transactions such as banking, brokerage, lending and investment inside electronic commerce, so the two labels are not alternatives.",
       "Range is exactly what the chapter contrasts fintech against. Narrowness is presented there as the working method, not as a disqualification."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical building society has accepted that its members now judge a two-working-day transfer against the payment apps they use for everything else. The board has to decide what follows from that, and it is split four ways. Which response fits the chapter&rsquo;s account of what fintech does to expectations?",
     opts:[
       "Benchmark the transfer against every other society in the region, and act only if the society turns out to be slower than its peers",
       "Publish an explanation of why two days is normal for a transfer of this kind, on the ground that an informed member expects less",
       "Treat the expectation as the standard the society is now held to, and decide whether to meet it or to compete where the apps are not",
       "Wait, since an expectation formed this quickly is likely to move again before the society could finish rebuilding anything at all"],
     a:2, obj:"4.1",
     why:[
       "Peer benchmarking answers a different question. This standard was set outside the industry, so the society can sit comfortably mid-table among societies and still lose members who have stopped comparing it with them.",
       "Explaining the mechanism does not undo the comparison. The chapter treats the reshaped expectation as a fact about customers rather than a misunderstanding a firm can talk them out of.",
       "Correct. The chapter says fintech services can greatly enhance customer experience yet also shape customer expectations, for instance in terms of ease of payment or speed. The expectation is therefore the standard the society is measured against, and the decision left is whether to meet it or to be better somewhere else.",
       "Nothing in the chapter suggests these expectations recede. They are described as an effect of services members already use daily, so waiting spends the interval and changes none of it."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical tool shop, Pinfold Tools, adds four extra fields and a telephone confirmation step to its checkout after a run of fraud. Fraud losses fall. Completed orders fall further, and most of the abandoned carts belonged to ordinary customers. What does the chapter&rsquo;s treatment of this trade suggest?",
     opts:[
       "Security has to be maximized whatever the effect on completion, because a fraudulent order costs the shop more than a lost order does",
       "The abandoned carts are unrelated to the checkout change, since the chapter attributes cart abandonment mainly to comparison shopping",
       "Telephone confirmation is the chapter&rsquo;s recommended default for every order, so a lower completion rate is simply the price of doing the job properly",
       "The shop should balance security against friction: collecting more data screens better, and it also drives shoppers away before they finish"],
     a:3, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter frames this as a balance rather than a maximum, and reports that more than half of online shopping carts are abandoned even before a step is added.",
       "Comparison shopping is one cause the chapter lists, along with security concerns, impatience and lengthy checkout procedures &mdash; which is precisely what has just been added here.",
       "The chapter describes calling a customer as something a merchant may do when the indicators warrant it, and notes that such calls can be problematic for privacy reasons.",
       "Correct. The chapter says it is essential to strike a balance between providing security and reducing friction, and notes that collecting as much data as possible during checkout may lead some customers to abandon their carts."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical map publisher, Peregrine Maps, is about to sell downloads from its own site. Its bookkeeper wants to store customer card numbers in the orders table so that repeat buyers do not have to retype them. A developer proposes accepting an established payment service instead. What is the substantive difference?",
     opts:[
       "There is none of substance: either way the publisher ends up holding the same payment details, and the service merely formats them for it",
       "The service settles every dispute on the publisher&rsquo;s behalf, which is the whole of the difference, since the card details are held in exactly the same way under both arrangements",
       "With a payment service the buyer gives sensitive details to the service alone, so the publisher never receives the card data it would otherwise have to protect",
       "A payment service is for transfers between people, so a publisher selling downloads would gain nothing at all by routing its sales through one"],
     a:2, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter&rsquo;s point is about where the information comes to rest. The customer has to provide the sensitive payment information only to the payment service.",
       "Payment services do sit between the parties, but the benefit the chapter states here is that the service keeps the information secure and does not share it with the merchant.",
       "Correct. Rather than paying a seller by providing card information, the shopper simply pays using their account with the payment service, which keeps that information and does not share it with the online merchant.",
       "One service the chapter describes also lets anyone with an email address send and receive money, but these same services are how a great many merchants take payment."]},

    {q:"Four hypothetical colleagues share a meal. One of them pays the whole bill by holding her phone against the restaurant&rsquo;s terminal, and the other three send her their shares from their own phones before they leave the table. Which description matches the chapter?",
     opts:[
       "Both movements of money are peer-to-peer payments, because a phone was used on both sides of each of them",
       "The bill was a mobile payment and the three reimbursements were peer-to-peer payments, carried by the same kind of service",
       "Neither is a payment service transaction, because no card number was typed into a website at any point in the evening",
       "The bill was a peer-to-peer payment and the reimbursements were business-to-consumer commerce settled inside a messaging app"],
     a:1, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The restaurant is a business taking payment at its own terminal, which the chapter treats as an in-store mobile payment rather than a transfer between two individuals.",
       "Correct. The chapter describes mobile payment services facilitating in-store payments as an alternative to carrying cash or cards, and a recent development allowing peer-to-peer payments so that friends can split the bill for a meal.",
       "The chapter&rsquo;s payment services cover in-store taps and phone-to-phone transfers; typing a card number into a web form is not what defines them.",
       "This reverses the two. Money going to the restaurant is the business transaction, and money moving between the diners is the transfer between individuals."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical furniture importer, Wexford Timber, already accepts a cryptocurrency to avoid card fees. Buyers ask what happens when goods arrive wrong, and the importer answers with a promise of its own: it will send the coins back whenever a buyer complains. Its finance lead thinks the promise closes the gap. What does it still leave the buyer without?",
     opts:[
       "Nothing at all, because a promise written by the seller does the same work for a buyer that a card company&rsquo;s guarantee does",
       "Any route back to the money, since an entry on the ledger cannot be deleted once the network has agreed to it",
       "Somebody other than the seller to settle the dispute, which is the part of the safety net the fee was buying",
       "A record of the payment, which is why a buyer paying this way has nothing to point at when a complaint is refused"],
     a:2, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "A promise the seller writes binds only the seller, and the buyer is left trusting the party they are in dispute with. That is the gap, not the wording of the promise.",
       "The coins can be sent back as a fresh transfer, so a refund is perfectly possible here. What cannot happen is a reversal imposed on a seller who has decided not to pay.",
       "Correct. The chapter says card companies and payment services act as trusted middlemen and provide consumers a safety net, giving confidence that a purchase will produce the desired good. A guarantee the seller issues and judges itself is not that.",
       "The ledger holds every transfer permanently and publicly, so evidence that the money moved is the one thing this buyer certainly does have."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical dealer in rare records tells a colleague that payments made in a cryptocurrency are anonymous and encrypted, so nobody can see who paid whom. The colleague looks up the ledger and finds every transfer listed there, going back years. Which correction is accurate?",
     opts:[
       "The ledger is encrypted, and what the colleague found was a separate public summary rather than the transactions themselves",
       "Transfers are pseudonymous rather than anonymous: the ledger is meant to be readable, and hashing and digital signatures are what secure it",
       "The ledger is anonymous but temporary, because entries are cleared once the majority of nodes have confirmed the block that holds them",
       "The dealer is right, and the colleague was reading a test network, since a live ledger is visible only to the computers that maintain it"],
     a:1, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter describes a secure public ledger that is accessible to any computer wanting to verify it, so openness here is the design rather than a leak.",
       "Correct. Addresses carry no names, but every transfer stays permanently visible and addresses can often be tied back to people, so pseudonymous is the accurate word. Authenticity comes from digital signatures and from the hashing that chains the blocks together, not from hiding the data.",
       "Entries are not cleared. The chapter calls the ledger indelible and says the chain serves as proof of all transactions ever made within the network.",
       "There is one shared public ledger in the chapter&rsquo;s account, constantly verified and maintained by the network and open every day of the year."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical audio brand, Quillon Audio, is told that because a cryptocurrency&rsquo;s network runs reliably and its fees are negligible, customers will use it as soon as the option appears at checkout. The finance lead is unconvinced. What does the chapter&rsquo;s record of merchant trials suggest?",
     opts:[
       "Adoption follows capability, so the option should be added and take-up will arrive along with it in due course",
       "Merchants abandoned it because the network could not settle payments quickly enough at the till, which is a technical fault rather than a question of what buyers are used to",
       "The chapter reports steady growth in purchases made this way, so the finance lead is arguing against the trend the evidence describes",
       "Several large sellers accepted it and later stopped, and purchase use stayed negligible, so a working mechanism is not an adopted one"],
     a:3, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter&rsquo;s account runs the other way: the capability existed, was offered by well-known sellers, and the buying public did not follow it.",
       "The chapter attributes the outcome to a lack of use rather than to a settlement failure, while noting that interest in the currency itself continued.",
       "The chapter records continued interest in the currency, but it describes use for making purchases as negligible rather than as growing steadily.",
       "Correct. The chapter reports that several major online companies announced they would accept the currency, that those trials have been terminated, and that its use for making purchase transactions has remained negligible."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical kayak maker, Duskwater Kayaks, receives an approved authorization on a large order and ships the same hour, telling staff that the approval confirms the buyer is the cardholder. Two weeks later the real cardholder disputes the charge. What did the approval actually establish, and what was thrown away by shipping at once?",
     opts:[
       "It established the buyer&rsquo;s identity, and nothing was thrown away, because a disputed transaction becomes the issuing bank&rsquo;s loss once that bank has approved it",
       "It established only that the card was not reported lost or stolen, and shipping at once used up the days before dispatch that could have gone on verification",
       "It established that the card was in the buyer&rsquo;s hands, and shipping at once made no difference, because the window runs from the order date",
       "It established that the billing and shipping addresses agree, and the maker gave up its chance to charge a higher price for overnight delivery"],
     a:1, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter says the merchant is financially responsible for disputed transactions, and that card issuers typically charge back transactions disputed by cardholders.",
       "Correct. Authorization merely ensures the card was not reported lost or stolen and does not ensure the person making the transaction is the actual cardholder. Because the transaction date is the date the merchandise ships, merchants typically have one or several days to verify identity &mdash; a window that does not exist for a digital product delivered at once.",
       "Possession is what the security code speaks to rather than the authorization, and the chapter ties the available days to the shipping date, not to the order date.",
       "Address agreement is one of the variables a screening service weighs. It is checked by the merchant rather than established by the issuer&rsquo;s approval."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical lighting supplier, Ombra Lighting, wants to keep the three-digit code from the back of each customer&rsquo;s card in its own customer file, so that returning buyers can skip a step and so that disputes can be re-checked later. What is wrong with the plan?",
     opts:[
       "Nothing, provided the file is encrypted, since the code is printed on the card and is not sensitive information in the chapter&rsquo;s account",
       "The code sits in the card&rsquo;s magnetic stripe already, so keeping a second copy adds risk without adding anything the supplier does not have",
       "Card company rules prohibit merchants from storing it, and storing it destroys what it proves: that someone had the physical card in hand",
       "The code changes with every transaction, so a stored copy would be stale before the next order and could not be re-checked against anything"],
     a:2, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter describes the code as a mechanism used to authorize card-not-present transactions, and it records an explicit prohibition on merchants keeping it.",
       "The chapter says the opposite: the code is not stored in the card&rsquo;s magnetic stripe, which is exactly why possession of the card is what it demonstrates.",
       "Correct. The chapter notes that credit card company rules prohibit merchants from storing the code, and that it is known only to a person who is in possession of the physical card.",
       "A printed code does not rotate between orders. Its value comes from where it is printed, and the rule against keeping it stands in any case."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical stationery seller, Larkfield Paper, builds its first storefront. It lifts the product photographs straight from its supplier&rsquo;s catalogue, and registers a domain with a well-known pen brand&rsquo;s name inside it because that is what buyers type into a search box. Its owner says nothing is wrong, since every item listed is genuine stock the shop has paid for. Where is the difficulty?",
     opts:[
       "Neither move is a problem, because the goods are genuine and the seller can show an invoice for every item it lists",
       "Only the domain is a problem, since a photograph published in a public catalogue may be reused by anyone selling the item shown in it",
       "Both are infringements in the chapter&rsquo;s own examples: the photograph is copyright, and the name in the domain is a trademark",
       "Only the photographs are a problem, and the domain is safe as long as the seller states its own jurisdiction clearly on the site"],
     a:2, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "Stocking the item settles nothing about whose intellectual property appears on the page. Whether the goods are genuine is a different question from who owns the photograph or the name.",
       "A catalogue photograph is a work somebody made and owns, and being able to see it transfers no right to publish it. Permission is what the chapter tells a seller to obtain first.",
       "Correct. The chapter calls ownership of content and trademarks a virtual minefield, and its two examples of infringing are exactly these: posting product photographs without permission, and having a trademarked name in your own domain name.",
       "Stating the jurisdiction and complying with it is separate housekeeping the chapter also asks for. It says nothing about whose mark sits inside the domain, and it makes that registration no safer."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical outdoor clothing seller, Ashgrove Outfitters, sets its system to reject any order whose shipping address differs from the billing address. Rejections rise, confirmed fraud falls a little, and the support inbox fills with people sending gifts or shipping to their workplace. How should the seller read that indicator?",
     opts:[
       "As a signal that should move a risk score alongside other variables, because a mismatch also describes a great many legitimate buyers",
       "As a test that settles the question, so the rule is right and the complaints are the unavoidable cost of controlling fraud",
       "As evidence that the screening service is miscalibrated, so the right response is to take address comparison out of the risk score altogether and rely on other variables",
       "As a reason to require telephone confirmation on every order, which takes the judgment out of the system and hands it to staff"],
     a:0, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "Correct. Address comparison is one variable among the time of the order, the transaction volume and the customer&rsquo;s network location; a refused genuine order costs the sale and often the customer as well, which is why one signal should not decide alone.",
       "The chapter presents address agreement as one of several variables feeding a risk score that the merchant then judges, not as a decision that stands on its own.",
       "Removing the variable discards real information. The chapter includes the match between shipping address, billing address and telephone number among the useful inputs.",
       "Calling every customer would be slow and costly, and the chapter notes that verification calls can be problematic for privacy reasons."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical online stationer trades from one small office and ships everywhere. Its owner argues that only the rules of the place where the office sits can apply to the business, because that is the only place it has any presence at all. What is the shape of the duty the chapter describes?",
     opts:[
       "Only the seller&rsquo;s own location matters, which is why sellers choose a home base with the lightest consumer rules available to them",
       "Consumer protection rules commonly attach to where the customer is, so many sellers work to the strictest requirements they face",
       "Consumer rules apply solely where the goods are physically delivered, so a download sold abroad falls outside every consumer regime",
       "A single harmonized international standard governs online selling, so one compliant policy answers every market the stationer ships to"],
     a:1, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter describes sellers choosing locations for tax reasons, but it treats consumer protection as attaching to the buyer rather than to the seller.",
       "Correct. The chapter says many consumer protection laws apply based on the location of the consumer, irrespective of whether a business has a presence in that location, which is why many sellers strive to comply with the strictest regulations they meet. Which rules bind a particular sale is a question to establish with somebody qualified in that jurisdiction.",
       "Delivery is not the hinge the chapter uses, and it describes the absence of physical interaction online as a reason these rules matter more rather than less.",
       "The chapter notes an attempt to harmonize rules within one bloc, while describing oversight elsewhere as split between a national body and state-level authorities."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical mail-order tea merchant has no office, warehouse or staff outside its own state, and has always told buyers elsewhere that no sales tax applies to them. Its sales into one neighboring state have grown steeply this year. Why might that growth change its obligations?",
     opts:[
       "Growth cannot change anything, since a duty to collect follows a physical presence and the merchant has no presence there",
       "The obligation would fall on the buyers as an import duty payable on arrival, so the merchant&rsquo;s own position is unchanged however fast its sales into that state grow",
       "A duty arises the moment any sale is made into another state, so the merchant has been wrong from the first order it ever shipped",
       "Crossing a sales threshold in a state can create a duty to collect there with no physical presence, which is why volume now matters"],
     a:3, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "The chapter describes exactly this rule changing: presence is no longer the only route by which a duty to collect can arise for an online seller.",
       "Import duty is the chapter&rsquo;s example for goods crossing a national border, not for a shipment moving between two states inside one country.",
       "The older treatment the chapter describes required a substantial presence, so the merchant&rsquo;s original position was not wrong at the time it was adopted.",
       "Correct. The chapter describes economic nexus, under which states can collect sales taxes from online sellers even without a physical location once certain sales thresholds are reached, and the tracking burden that follows. What any particular threshold is now is a question to establish with somebody qualified in that jurisdiction."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical audiobook publisher is deciding whether to lock its files so that they play only on registered devices and only a set number of times. Its editor is for the idea and its head of support is against it. What is the argument on each side, as the chapter presents them?",
     opts:[
       "For: locking discourages unauthorized copying and protects sales. Against: the restrictions inconvenience buyers, and some firms succeed without them",
       "For: locked files load faster on a registered device. Against: the encoding cost is high and rises with the size of the catalog",
       "For: the law requires publishers to restrict copying. Against: enforcement is impossible once a file has left the publisher&rsquo;s servers",
       "For: watermarking each file identifies the buyer. Against: watermarks reduce audio quality enough for listeners to notice the difference"],
     a:0, obj:"4.5",
     why:[
       "Correct. The chapter says the entertainment industry argues these controls minimize sales losses by preventing unauthorized duplication, while critics say publishers are arbitrary in how they enforce them, that limits on devices and activations inconvenience users and can breed piracy, and that some businesses do very well without them.",
       "Loading speed is not part of the argument. The chapter frames the technology as a control on copying and distribution rather than as a performance feature.",
       "The chapter presents this as a commercial choice publishers make, not a legal requirement, and it names successful sellers who have chosen the other way.",
       "Watermarking is the chapter&rsquo;s alternative for unlocked files, used to trace an illegal copy back to the original purchaser rather than to prevent playback."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical maker of wooden toys builds a storefront on a hosted commerce platform, and after three months has had forty visitors. Its founder concludes that the platform was badly built. What has the founder misread about the layers the supplement describes?",
     opts:[
       "Nothing at all: a storefront platform is sold as a complete solution to selling online, so weak traffic is a defect in the product the maker paid for",
       "A storefront platform builds and runs the shop; attracting customers to it is a separate problem, which is why sellers turn to marketplaces",
       "The platform layer includes fulfillment, so the founder should be measuring deliveries rather than visits during a first quarter of trading",
       "Payment providers are the layer that brings demand, so the founder should change the checkout options before touching anything else"],
     a:1, obj:"4.6",
     why:[
       "The supplement separates the layers precisely because no single one of them covers the whole job of selling something online.",
       "Correct. Building and running your own online store still requires attracting customers to your website, which is a challenge for a fledgling business; that gap is what a marketplace with an existing customer base fills.",
       "Fulfillment is a separate layer in the supplement, handled by third-party providers managing warehousing, shipping and returns rather than by the storefront software.",
       "Payment providers reduce friction at checkout for buyers who have already arrived. They do not bring anyone to a shop that nobody has found."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical soap maker lists on a large marketplace and its orders multiply. A year later it wants to email those buyers about a new range, and finds that it cannot reach most of them. What was the trade it made?",
     opts:[
       "It borrowed the marketplace&rsquo;s audience and technology, and the marketplace kept the customer relationship that the audience produced",
       "It bought advertising space, so the customers were rented for the length of the campaign and returned to the marketplace afterwards",
       "It became a business-to-business seller, so the buyers are the marketplace&rsquo;s customers and the soap maker&rsquo;s single customer is the marketplace",
       "It disintermediated its own shop, so the marketplace is now the one route by which any of its products can reach a buyer"],
     a:0, obj:"4.6",
     why:[
       "Correct. A marketplace lends a seller its customer base and its technology, connecting a small seller with a very large audience at once; what it does not hand over is the relationship with the people who buy.",
       "Advertising buys attention for a period. A marketplace listing is a sales channel, and the limit here is on contact afterwards rather than on the length of a campaign.",
       "The people buying soap are consumers. Paying a marketplace a fee does not turn a retail sale into trade between two businesses.",
       "Disintermediation means removing a middleman. The soap maker added one, and nothing stops it from continuing to sell through its own site as well."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical seller of climbing holds packs orders in a rented garage and turns away a spike of demand every autumn, because it cannot pick and post fast enough. A third-party provider quotes a per-order fee higher than the seller&rsquo;s own handling cost. When does that fee stop being simply an expense?",
     opts:[
       "It does not, because a fee above the seller&rsquo;s own cost per order is a worse deal on every single order it touches",
       "When the provider agrees to hold the stock at no charge, since warehousing is the part of fulfillment worth handing over",
       "Once the seller has grown enough to build its own warehouse, at which point the fee becomes an investment in the eventual move",
       "When it stops being a comparison of fees and becomes the purchase of delivery speed and capacity the seller cannot fund itself"],
     a:3, obj:"4.6",
     why:[
       "Cost per order is not the whole comparison. The supplement points at speed, at scale, and at the orders the seller currently cannot accept at all.",
       "Warehousing is one part of the service. The providers described also handle stock, shipping and returns, and speed of delivery is a large part of the value.",
       "Building a warehouse is the significant upfront investment the arrangement is meant to avoid, so a fee paid now is not a deposit against one.",
       "Correct. Third-party providers offer rapid delivery, warehousing, shipping and returns handling, and scalable logistics without significant upfront investment &mdash; which is what a garage cannot supply at the autumn peak."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical piano dealer finds customers playing instruments in its showroom and then ordering the same model online for less. It is weighing three responses: matching online prices, offering fitting and tuning that a website cannot, and putting sensors in the aisles to track how long shoppers linger. What does the supplement say about the third one?",
     opts:[
       "Tracking is the strongest of the three, because measurement is what lets the other two responses be judged and adjusted over time",
       "Price matching and personal service address the price gap directly, while tracking raises a privacy objection the dealer has to answer openly",
       "Tracking is equivalent to price matching, because both are ways of collecting more value from a shopper who is already inside the shop",
       "Tracking is required before price matching can be offered, since a dealer has to know which shoppers were comparing before it can match anything"],
     a:1, obj:"4.6",
     why:[
       "Measurement can be useful, but the supplement presents service and price matching as the answers to showrooming and treats in-store tracking as a separate question with objections attached to it.",
       "Correct. Retailers answer showrooming with price-matching policies and superior, personal customer service, benefiting by selling additional products, while privacy advocates criticize the tracking of purchasing habits, so the dealer has to say plainly what is collected and why.",
       "The two do different work. One closes the price gap the shopper walked in with; the other gathers information about the shopper and needs consent to be defensible.",
       "A price-matching policy is published and applies to anyone who asks for it. It does not depend on identifying which shoppers have been comparing prices."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical bicycle shop&rsquo;s written analysis opens with one line: sales are down, and customers complain about the website. The writer then moves straight on to a recommendation. Which step of the method has been skipped, and what would repair it?",
     opts:[
       "It names no numbers, so setting last year&rsquo;s revenue beside each complaint would repair it",
       "It is written in the first person, so restating the two observations in a neutral voice would repair it",
       "These are symptoms: step two asks which pressure is acting on the shop, and on what evidence",
       "It covers one force only, so a sentence about each of the other four would repair it"],
     a:2, obj:"4.7",
     why:[
       "Counting belongs to step one, whose stronger version is a delivered competitor price set beside your own shelf price. A number attached to a symptom is still a symptom.",
       "Voice is a matter of style. What is missing is the pressure itself, which is what the second step of the method exists to supply.",
       "Correct. Step two is to name the pressure and say which side of the firm it is acting on. The weak-against-stronger table sets &ldquo;customers are leaving the basket&rdquo; against &ldquo;buyer power is high, because comparing prices costs the customer nothing&rdquo;, and this analysis has written only the weak version.",
       "Four more sentences would still name no pressure a reader could argue with. The step is finished when a force is named with evidence behind it, not when five headings have been filled in."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical dairy&rsquo;s analysis names two areas where a system would help: the website, and the online shop. Each description runs to a single sentence, and both of them point at the same ordering screen. What is the defect?",
     opts:[
       "Two entries, one place: the chain has been located once, so nothing has been learned about the rest of it",
       "The entries are too short, so expanding each of them into a paragraph would repair it without changing what they name",
       "Neither entry is a system, so the writer should have named the software product it intends to buy for each of the two areas",
       "Ordering is a support activity rather than a primary one, so it sits outside the part of the chain worth locating"],
     a:0, obj:"4.7",
     why:[
       "Correct. Step three is to locate the pressure somewhere in the value chain, and two names for one ordering screen locate it in a single place rather than in two.",
       "Length is not the failure here. A longer description of the same ordering screen would still point at a single place in the chain.",
       "Naming a product belongs to the recommendation. Locating is about where in the chain the pressure sits, which is settled before anyone decides what to buy.",
       "Support activities carry pressures too: this supplement maps compliance duties into firm infrastructure. The category of the activity is not what makes this pair weak."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical optician&rsquo;s analysis ends with three ideas &mdash; online booking, a loyalty scheme, and a stock system &mdash; and invites the reader to choose between them. Each idea is described in a phrase. What does the analysis need instead?",
     opts:[
       "The same three ideas ranked by cost, so that the reader can see which is cheapest to start with and choose on that basis",
       "A fourth idea drawn from a different part of the chain, so that the reader has a wider set of options to weigh before deciding for themselves",
       "The three ideas described at greater length, since the weakness is the phrase-length treatment rather than the number of ideas offered",
       "One initiative, with the pressure it answers, what it changes, what it costs, and what you would watch afterwards"],
     a:3, obj:"4.7",
     why:[
       "Ranking still hands the decision back to the reader, and the fourth step of the method asks the writer to make the call and then stand behind it.",
       "Adding an option moves in the wrong direction. The difficulty is that no choice has been made, not that too few were listed.",
       "Length would improve each description, but three described ideas with no recommendation among them is still a menu rather than a recommendation.",
       "Correct. Step four is to recommend one thing and say what it changes, what it costs and what you would watch afterwards to know whether it worked. The table sets three uncosted initiatives against exactly that, and the two ideas you drop can be named as deliberately not chosen."]},

    {q:"A hypothetical bookbinder&rsquo;s comparison section says that a very large retailer built a network of automated warehouses, and concludes that the bookbinder should build automated warehouses too. The section runs to ninety words. Name what is wrong with it.",
     opts:[
       "The lesson only works at the larger firm&rsquo;s budget and footprint, and the section sits well below the length the task sets",
       "The comparison is with a real company, which the task does not permit, so an invented example of a similar size would have been safer",
       "The lesson is sound and the single defect is length, so extending the section past the lower bound would finish the job",
       "The company chosen is in a different industry, and a comparison has to be drawn from the same trade to carry any weight"],
     a:0, obj:"4.7",
     why:[
       "Correct. A lesson that only holds at the larger company&rsquo;s budget or footprint is not a lesson that transfers, and the comparison is set at a hundred and fifty to two hundred words, which ninety does not reach.",
       "Nothing in the method turns on whether the comparison company is real. What has to work is the lesson drawn back to the firm you are advising.",
       "Length is one of the two faults. A longer passage recommending warehouse automation to a bookbinder would still be recommending something out of reach.",
       "Industry is not what decides it. A comparison fails when the lesson does not transfer, and warehouse automation does not survive the move to a bookbinder&rsquo;s scale and footprint."]}

  ]
};
